<odoo>
    <data noupdate="0">
        <record id="group_whatsapp_user" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">WhatsApp: send</field>
            <field name="comment">Can send WhatsApp messages and read the log.</field>
        </record>
        <record id="group_whatsapp_admin" model="res.groups">
            <field name="name">WhatsApp: configure</field>
            <field name="implied_ids" eval="[(4, ref('group_whatsapp_user'))]"/>
            <field name="comment">Owns the senders and the templates. The access token is
                a paid credential, so configuring is deliberately separate from sending.</field>
        </record>
        <!-- WHOSE MESSAGES ARE WHOSE (client, 2026-09-19) - in security/ir.access.csv.
             There were no rules on the message at all, so anybody with "WhatsApp: send"
             could read every message the company had ever sent, for every contact. A sender
             sees the ones that are theirs; configuring sees the lot.

             "Theirs" is three things, because the model already means three: user_id is
             who SHOULD send it (empty meaning anybody with the right, which is the shared
             queue and must stay visible or nobody sends it), and sent_by_id is who
             actually DID (rule_whatsapp_message_own).

             Odoo 20 OR-s every permission row a user's groups give them. Odoo 19's
             read-only ACL for every internal user (for the record smart buttons of
             business modules) carried no rule, so a sender - also an internal user -
             still saw only their own; as a plain permission row in 20 it would hand
             every sender every message again. So that row gives nothing to a sender
             (their own row decides) and stays all-records for an internal user who
             does not send, exactly as in 19. -->
        <record id="base.user_admin" model="res.users">
            <field name="group_ids" eval="[(4, ref('group_whatsapp_admin'))]"/>
        </record>
    </data>
</odoo>
